{ Black against 1.e4 e5. You answer 1.e4 with ...e5 and meet the Italian, the Three Knights, the Scotch, the Vienna and the Bishop's Opening. In each, aim for quick development, an equal share of the centre and a safe king. You play Black. }
1. e4
{ [%eval 0.34] White takes space in the centre and opens lines for the queen and the f1 bishop. Black does not need to copy it to be fine, but ...e5 is the most direct reply. }
1... e5
{ [%eval 0.37] [%cal Ge7e5] Black claims an equal share of the centre. Most of the openings you will meet as Black start from this reply. }
2. Nf3
{ [%eval 0.36] Develops a knight and attacks the pawn on e5. By far the most common move. }
( 2. Nc3
{ [%eval 0.11] The Vienna. White develops without committing the f1 knight, and keeps ideas of f4 in hand. }
2... Nf6 { [%eval 0.10] Develops and attacks e4. } 3. f4
{ [%eval -0.26] The Vienna Gambit. White uses the open f-file at the cost of a pawn. }
( 3. Bc4 { [%eval -0.05] The Vienna with Bc4. White aims at f7 again. } 3...
Nxe4
{ [%eval 0.13] The right reply. Black takes the pawn and invites 4.Nxe4 d5, which wins a tempo for Black. }
4. Qh5 { [%eval 0.07] White threatens f7 with the queen and the bishop. } 4...
Nd6
{ [%eval 0.15] The knight retreats to a safe square and keeps the extra pawn. Be careful about f7: the queen and the bishop both aim at it, so Black's next moves have to deal with the threat of Qxf7 mate. }
5. Bb3 { [%eval 0.06] The bishop retreats and keeps the queen's attack on e5. }
5... Be7 { [%eval 0.03] Develops and prepares to castle. } 6. Nf3
{ [%eval -0.21] White develops and attacks e5 a second time. } 6... Nc6
{ [%eval -0.15] Defends e5 again. } 7. Nxe5 { [%eval -0.24] White takes. } 7...
Nxe5 { [%eval 0.35] Black takes. } 8. Qxe5
{ [%eval 0.38] White takes back and the queen is strong in the centre, so Black must be ready for tricks on the e-file. }
8... O-O $14 { [%eval 0.36] Black castles and has a fine game. } ) 3... d5
{ [%eval -0.26] [%cal Gd7d5] Strikes at the centre. Do not take on f4 with 3...exf4?, because 4.e5 kicks the knight and Black is worse placed. Hitting back with ...d5 is the same idea as the Falkbeer Countergambit in the King's Gambit. }
4. fxe5 { [%eval -0.26] White takes. } 4... Nxe4
{ [%eval -0.23] Black takes back and has a strong centre knight. } 5. Nf3
{ [%eval -0.27] White develops and defends e5. } 5... Be7
{ [%eval 0.00] The Breyer Variation, a solid setup. White gets open lines and kingside chances, so Black often looks for counterplay on the queenside with ...c5 and ...Qa5. Castling comes first. }
6. d4 { [%eval -0.27] White builds the centre. } 6... O-O $10
{ [%eval 0.13] Black castles. The position is level. } ) ( 2. Bc4
{ [%eval 0.12] The Bishop's Opening. The bishop comes out before the knight and aims at f7. }
2... Nf6
{ [%eval 0.16] The Berlin Defence, Black's most active reply: it attacks e4 at once and forces White to defend it. Black must watch for a delayed f4, which can turn the game into a kind of King's Gambit. }
3. d3 { [%eval 0.22] Supports e4 and keeps the position closed. } ( 3. Nc3
{ [%eval -0.06] White develops the knight and plans f4. } 3... Nxe4
{ [%eval 0.13] Black takes the pawn. This is the way to answer 3.Nc3: White gets only a trade of pieces. }
4. Nxe4 { [%eval -0.87] White takes the knight. } 4... d5
{ [%eval -0.74] Forks the bishop and the knight. } 5. Bd3
{ [%eval -0.77] White defends the knight and takes the fork away. } 5... dxe4
{ [%eval -0.83] Black takes the knight. } 6. Bxe4
{ [%eval -0.85] White takes the pawn back and the material is level. } 6... Nc6
$10 { [%eval -0.09] Develops and eyes d4. Black has an easy game. } ) 3... c6
{ [%eval 0.20] [%cal Gc6d5] The Paulsen Defence. Black builds a strong centre of ...c6, ...d5 and ...e5, and the bishop must move again, which gains a tempo. White's counterplay is to attack the chain. }
